===March===
[[File:Dodgers ring ceremony 2026.jpg|thumb|The Dodgers received their 2025 World Series rings on March 27 before their game against the Diamondbacks.]]
The Dodgers began the season with a three-game series against the [[Arizona Diamondbacks]] at the newly re-named [[Dodger Stadium|Uniqlo Field at Dodger Stadium]]. [[Yoshinobu Yamamoto]] made his second straight [[opening day]] start, allowing two runs on five hits with six strikeouts while [[Andy Pages]] and [[Will Smith (catcher)|Will Smith]] each homered in the 8–2 win.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/andy-pages-homers-in-dodgers-2026-opening-day-victory?game_pk=823974|title=Pages' Opening Day homer good starting point in pivotal year|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=March 26, 2026|access-date=March 26, 2026}}</ref> The following day, the Dodgers scored four runs in the third on a solo homer by [[Alex Freeland]] and a three-run homer by [[Mookie Betts]]. The game was tied when [[Kyle Tucker]] drove in Freeland with the eventual winning run as the Dodgers won 5–4.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/kyle-tucker-edwin-diaz-lead-dodgers-past-d-backs?game_pk=823971|title='Game-changing' new Dodgers Tucker, Díaz live up to billing|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=March 28, 2026|access-date=March 28, 2026}}</ref> They finished off the opening series sweep with a 3–2 win in the series finale. [[Tyler Glasnow]] allowed two runs in six innings with six strikeouts and the Dodgers took the lead with a two-run home run by Smith in the eighth inning.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/dodgers/news/will-smith-hits-go-ahead-home-run-on-birthday-bobblehead-night|title=Big HR on bobblehead night -- all in a (birth)day's work for Dodgers' Smith|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=March 29, 2026|access-date=March 29, 2026}}</ref> The [[Cleveland Guardians]] came to town next and handed the Dodgers their first loss of the season, 4–2.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/parker-messick-goes-6-scoreless-innings-vs-dodgers?game_pk=823972|title=Messick looks like a veteran, holds champs scoreless through 6|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=March 30, 2026|access-date=March 31, 2026}}</ref> In the following game, [[Shohei Ohtani]] allowed only one hit while striking out six in six scoreless innings as the Dodgers won 4–1.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/shohei-ohtani-6-scoreless-innings-2026-pitching-debut?game_pk=823969|title=Ohtani makes it look easy in 2026 pitching debut|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=March 31, 2026|access-date=April 1, 2026}}</ref>

===April===
In the second game of the series, Yamamoto allowed two runs in six innings but Cleveland got home runs from [[Gabriel Arias (infielder)|Gabriel Arias]] in the third and [[José Ramírez (third baseman)|José Ramírez]] in the eighth to win 4–1. The Dodgers became the first major league team to have three Japanese-born pitchers in consecutive games ([[Roki Sasaki]], Ohtani and Yamamoto).<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/dodgers/news/yoshinobu-yamamoto-shohei-ohtani-roki-sasaki-make-history-with-dodgers|title=LA first team to throw three Japanese-born starters in a row|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=April 1, 2026|access-date=April 2, 2026}}</ref>

The Dodgers went on their first road trip of the season on April 3 to play the [[Washington Nationals]] at [[Nationals Park]]. [[Emmet Sheehan]] allowed four runs in {{fraction|5|2|3}} innings while the Dodgers hit five home runs in a 13–6 win.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/dodgers/news/shohei-ohtani-hits-his-first-home-run-of-2026|title=That'll do it! Ohtani's 1st homer ignites rest of lineup amid slower start|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=April 3, 2026|access-date=April 3, 2026}}</ref> The following day, Glasnow allowed two runs while striking out nine in six innings, Pages had three hits (including a three run home run) and [[Freddie Freeman]] had two doubles and four RBI in a 10–5 win.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/andy-pages-three-run-homer-three-hits-to-lead-dodgers?game_pk=822757|title=This Dodger has more hits than whiffs -- and he's homered on back-to-back days!|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=April 4, 2026|access-date=April 5, 2026}}</ref> In the series finale, the Dodgers scored four runs in the eighth inning to come from behind and finish off the sweep with a 8–6 win.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/roki-sasaki-allows-six-runs-dodgers-rally-win-vs-nationals?game_pk=822756|title=Big bats back Sasaki's stumbles, secure sweep vs. Nats|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=April 5, 2026|access-date=April 5, 2026}}</ref> Next up was a three-game series against the [[Toronto Blue Jays]] at [[Rogers Centre]], a rematch of the [[2025 World Series]]. [[Justin Wrobleski]] started the opener for the Dodgers, allowing one run on two hits in five innings. [[Dalton Rushing]] had four hits (including two home runs) while the Dodgers hit three additional homers in a 14–2 win.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/dalton-rushing-dodgers-win-blue-jays-series-opener?game_pk=822832|title=Rushing's 2-HR, 4-hit game fuels Dodgers' dominant return to Toronto|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=April 6, 2026|access-date=April 6, 2026}}</ref> Yamamoto allowed only one run in six innings the following day as the Dodgers won, 4–1.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/yoshinobu-yamamoto-strikes-out-six-blue-jays-in-return-to-toronto?game_pk=822831|title=Yamamoto ignores boos, subdues Blue Jays yet again in Toronto encore|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=April 7, 2026|access-date=April 7, 2026}}</ref> In the series finale, Ohtani allowed only one unearned run in six innings but the Blue Jays scored three runs in the seventh and eighth off the Dodgers bullpen to beat them 4–3.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/blue-jays-come-back-to-beat-dodgers-snap-six-game-losing-streak?game_pk=822833|title=Blue Jays' rally over Dodgers a sign of life from gritty '25 identity|work=mlb.com|first=Keegan|last=Matheson|date=April 8, 2026|access-date=April 9, 2026}}</ref>
[[File:Max Muncy May 20, 2018 (50121480882).jpg|thumb|left|Muncy hit three home runs in a game on April 10.]]
Back in Los Angeles on April 10, against the [[Texas Rangers (baseball)|Texas Rangers]], Pages had three hits, including a double and a home run, and [[Max Muncy]] hit three home runs, including a [[Walk-off home run|walk-off]] as the Dodgers won 8–7. He was only the second Dodger to hit three and a walk-off, joining [[Don Demeter]], who did it in [[1959 Los Angeles Dodgers season|1959]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/max-muncy-hits-three-home-runs-dodgers-walk-off-rangers?game_pk=823968|title=Muncy climbs all-time ranks with historic 3-HR night in LA|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=April 11, 2026|access-date=April 11, 2026}}</ref> In the next game, the Dodgers scored four runs in the first inning on a lead-off homer by Ohtani and a three-run homer by [[Teoscar Hernández]] and they won the game 6–3.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/shohei-ohtani-leadoff-homer-extends-on-base-streak-to-45-games?game_pk=823967|title=Ohtani's leadoff blast sets tone as on-base streak hits 45 games|work=mlb.com|first=Courtney|last=Hollman|date=April 11, 2026|access-date=April 12, 2026}}</ref> The Rangers won the final game of the series, 5–2. [[Jacob deGrom]] allowed only one run in six innings while striking out nine.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/jacob-degrom-has-nine-strikeouts-rangers-win-vs-dodgers?game_pk=823966|title=deGrom, the Dodger dominator? Ace K's 9 in another gem vs. LA|work=mlb.com|first=Jacob|last=Gurvis|date=April 12, 2026|access-date=April 12, 2026}}</ref> The next series brought the [[New York Mets]] to Los Angeles. Wrobleski allowed only two hits in eight scoreless innings and Pages hit a three-run home run in a 4–0 Dodgers victory to start things off.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/justin-wrobleski-throws-eight-scoreless-innings-against-mets?game_pk=823964|title=Wrobleski dials up career-best outing with 8 scoreless vs. Mets|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=April 13, 2026|access-date=April 14, 2026}}</ref> In the next game, Yamamoto allowed only one run (a solo home run by [[Francisco Lindor]] to open the game) on four hits in {{fraction|7|1|3}} innings while striking out seven. [[Nolan McLean]] matched him with one run on two hits in seven innings with eight strikeouts. The Dodgers won 2–1 on an RBI single by [[Kyle Tucker]] in the eighth inning.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/yoshinobu-yamamoto-retires-20-in-a-row-against-mets?game_pk=823965|title='Vintage Yamamoto' as LA ace shakes off leadoff HR, retires 20 straight|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=April 15, 2026|access-date=April 15, 2026}}</ref> The following day, Ohtani allowed one run on two hits in six innings with 10 strike outs, Rushing hit a grand slam home run and the Dodgers finished off a sweep with a 8–2 win.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/shohei-ohtani-strikes-out-10-as-streak-without-allowing-earned-run-ends?game_pk=823963|title=Focusing solely on pitching, Ohtani fans 10 in 6 innings as L.A. completes sweep|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=April 15, 2026|access-date=April 16, 2026}}</ref>

The Dodgers next traveled to [[Coors Field]] to play the [[Colorado Rockies]], beginning on April 17. Glasnow struck out seven while allowing only two hits and one run in seven innings while Muncy hit two home runs in a 7–1 Dodgers win.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/tyler-glasnow-pitches-gem-in-dodgers-win-at-cold-coors-field?game_pk=824373|title=Glasnow, Dodgers stay hot in dominant win over Rockies at frigid Coors Field|work=mlb.com|first=Manny|last=Randhawa|date=April 17, 2026|access-date=April 18, 2026}}</ref> Next, the Dodgers took a 3–2 lead after the second inning thanks to home runs by Tucker and Rushing, but the Rockies came back to score two in the sixth and held on to win 4–3.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/jaden-hill-s-relief-performance-in-rockies-win?game_pk=824372|title=Hill's clutch relief performance lifts Rox over LA -- and shows his growth|work=mlb.com|first=Thomas|last=Harding|date=April 18, 2026|access-date=April 19, 2026}}</ref> The Rockies won again the next day, 9–6, handing the Dodgers their first back-to-back losses of the season.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/mickey-moniak-edouard-julien-lead-rockies-to-win-over-dodgers?game_pk=824371|title=Moniak, Julien lead offensive outburst as Rockies hand Dodgers 2nd straight loss|work=mlb.com|first=Thomas|last=Harding|date=April 19, 2026|access-date=April 19, 2026}}</ref> The Dodgers did win the follow day, to gain a split of the series with the Rockies, 12–3. Wrobleski allowed one run in seven innings and both Muncy and Rushing had two home runs in the game.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/max-muncy-hits-2-home-runs-miguel-rojas-hits-1-in-dodgers-win?game_pk=824370|title='Just had a feeling': Roberts flips Miggy and Muncy in order -- and they go off for 3 homers|work=mlb.com|first=Owen|last=Perkins|date=April 20, 2026|access-date=April 21, 2026}}</ref> On April 22, the Dodgers began a three game series against the [[San Francisco Giants]] at [[Oracle Park]]. In the opener, the Giants held the Dodgers offense in check in a 3–1 win.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/landen-roupp-sharp-in-giants-win-over-dodgers?game_pk=823236|title=Roupp's gem, bullpen lift Giants past Dodgers in opener|work=mlb.com|first=Maria|last=Guardado|date=April 21, 2026|access-date=April 22, 2026}}</ref> The next game, Ohtani struck out seven in six scoreless innings but a three-run home run by [[Patrick Bailey]] in the seventh was all the Giants needed in a 3–0 win. Ohtani's season-opening on base streak ended at 53 consecutive games, which tied the Los Angeles era franchise record set by [[Shawn Green]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/dodgers/news/shohei-ohtani-on-base-streak-ends-at-53-games|title=Ohtani's on-base streak ends as Dodgers blanked for 1st time in 2026|work=MLB.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=April 22, 2026|access-date=April 23, 2026}}</ref> The Dodgers avoided a sweep with a 3–0 win of their own in the finale. Glasnow struck out nine and allowed only one hit in eight scoreless innings.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/tyler-glasnow-pitches-8-scoreless-innings-to-beat-giants?game_pk=823233|title=Glasnow rolls with dominant 8 innings to lead Dodgers past Giants|work=MLB.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=April 23, 2026|access-date=April 24, 2026}}</ref>

Back home on April 24, the Dodgers took on the [[Chicago Cubs]] in a three-game series. [[Emmet Sheehan]] held the Cubs to only one run in {{fraction|6|1|3}} innings while striking out 10 batters while [[Will Smith (catcher)|Will Smith]] hit a three-run home run in the third to give the Dodgers an early lead. However, the Cubs came back against the Dodgers bullpen and a two-run homer by [[Dansby Swanson]] in the ninth gave them a 6–4 win.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/dodgers/news/dodgers-bullpen-struggles-late-in-loss-to-cubs|title=Dodgers' trio of high-leverage arms overpowered by surging Cubs|work=MLB.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=April 24, 2026|access-date=April 25, 2026}}</ref> In the following game, the Dodgers scored 12 runs on 14 hits in a 12–4 victory.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/dodgers-score-12-runs-to-end-cubs-winning-streak?game_pk=823960|title=Dodgers drop a dozen on Cubs in long-awaited offensive outburst|work=MLB.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=April 25, 2026|access-date=April 26, 2026}}</ref> Ohtani had three hits, including a home run, in the series finale while Wrobleski pitched six scoreless innings in the Dodgers 6–0 win.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/shohei-ohtani-3-hits-in-dodgers-shutout?game_pk=823959|title=Ohtani tallies first 3-hit game of '26 as Dodgers shut out Cubs in finale|work=MLB.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=April 26, 2026|access-date=April 26, 2026}}</ref> The [[Miami Marlins]] came to town next and a [[Liam Hicks]] three-run home run off Yamamoto in the fifth inning gave them a 4–2 lead going into the ninth. However, a ground-rule double by Ohtani and a two-run single by Tucker led to a 5–4 walkoff win for the Dodgers.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/kyle-tucker-hits-walk-off-single-to-beat-marlins?game_pk=823961|title='He needed it': King Tuck's LA reign finally begins with 2-out, 2-RBI walk-off|work=MLB.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=April 28, 2026|access-date=April 28, 2026}}</ref> The following day, Ohtani struck out nine Marlins in six innings but the Dodgers lost 2–1.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/dodgers/news/shohei-ohtani-limits-marlins-to-one-earned-run-in-six-innings|title=Ohtani strikes out 9 Marlins, but offense misses his bat|work=MLB.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=April 28, 2026|access-date=April 29, 2026}}</ref> The Marlins took the series with a 3–2 win in the series finale. An RBI single by [[Javier Sanoja]] in the eighth was the go-ahead run.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/marlins-win-first-series-at-dodger-stadium-since-2018?game_pk=823958|title=Marlins outslug Dodgers for first series win in LA in 8 years|work=MLB.com|first=Christina|last=De Nicola|date=April 29, 2026|access-date=April 30, 2026}}</ref>

===May===
The Dodgers began the month of May by playing the [[St. Louis Cardinals]] at [[Busch Stadium]]. In the opener, a [[Nolan Gorman]] home run in the first gave the Cardinals a 3–0 lead and they never looked back, winning 7–2.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/nolan-gorman-alec-burleson-homer-in-cardinals-win-over-dodgers?game_pk=823066|title=Cardinals' surprising power surge has earned them a new HR celebration|date=May 1, 2026|access-date=May 3, 2026|first=Brenden|last=Schaeffer |website=[[MLB.com]] }}</ref> In the next game, a [[Alec Burleson]] double and a [[Jordan Walker (baseball)|Jordan Walker]] home run in the third inning also gave the Cardinals a 3–0 lead as they won again, 3–2 and handed the Dodgers their fourth straight loss.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/dodgers/news/dodgers-lose-4th-straight-despite-roki-sasaki-s-strong-start|title=Dodgers hope 9th-inning rally is sign of progress despite 4th straight loss|date=May 2, 2026|access-date=May 3, 2026|first=Sonja|last=Chen |website=[[MLB.com]] }}</ref> [[Justin Wrobleski]] pitched six scoreless innings on May 3 as the Dodgers won 4–1 to snap the losing streak.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/dodgers-snap-4-game-skid-behind-justin-wrobleski-s-strong-start?game_pk=823065|title=Dodgers win series finale behind Wrobleski to snap 4-game skid|date=May 3, 2026|access-date=May 3, 2026|first=Sonja|last=Chen |website=[[MLB.com]] }}</ref> Next up was the [[Houston Astros]] at [[Daikin Park]]. [[Alex Freeland]] had three hits, including a home run, and [[Kyle Tucker]] had two hits and a home run as the Dodgers won 8–3.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/yoshinobu-yamamoto-faces-astros-for-the-first-time-in-his-career?game_pk=824201|title=Yamamoto (8 K's) rebounds as Dodgers' offense comes back to life in Houston|date=May 4, 2026|access-date=May 5, 2026|first=Courtney|last=Hollman |website=[[MLB.com]] }}</ref> [[Shohei Ohtani]] struck out eight in seven innings in the following game but allowed two solo home runs, which was enough to sink the Dodgers, 2–1.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/dodgers/news/shohei-ohtani-allows-first-homers-of-2026|title=Ohtani goes 7 IP for 1st time in '26, but also allows 1st HRs|date=May 5, 2026|access-date=May 6, 2026|first=Courtney|last=Hollman |website=[[MLB.com]] }}</ref> The Dodgers finished out the series with a 12–2 victory over the Astros on May 6. [[Andy Pages]] hit three home runs in the game.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/dodgers/news/andy-pages-hits-three-home-runs-in-dodgers-win-vs-astros|title=What can't he do? Pages homers 3 times AND shows off glove in Dodgers' rout|date=May 6, 2026|access-date=May 6, 2026|first=Courtney|last=Hollman |website=[[MLB.com]] }}</ref>

Back home on May 8, the Dodgers beat the [[Atlanta Braves]], 3–1. [[Freddie Freeman]] hit his 100th home run as a Dodger in the game.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/freddie-freeman-hits-100th-dodgers-home-run?game_pk=823957|title=Freeman hits 100th Dodgers homer -- against his former club|access-date=May 9, 2026|date=May 9, 2026|first=Courtney|last=Hollman |website=[[MLB.com]] }}</ref> [[Blake Snell]] made his first start of the season on May 9, after starting the season on the injured list. It did not go well as he allowed five runs on six hits in three innings as the Braves won 7–2.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/dodgers/news/blake-snell-makes-2026-season-debut-for-dodgers|title=Back ahead of schedule, Snell's 2026 debut a mixed bag|access-date=May 10, 2026|date=May 9, 2026|first=Sonja|last=Chen |website=[[MLB.com]] }}</ref> They also lost by an identical, 7–2 score the next day.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/dodgers/news/justin-wrobleski-goes-8-2-3-innings-in-dodgers-loss|title=Wrobleski preserves 'pen with 8 2/3-inning battle but bats still in a 'funk'|access-date=May 10, 2026|date=May 10, 2026|first=Sonja|last=Chen |website=[[MLB.com]] }}</ref> The Dodgers woes continued as the [[San Francisco Giants]] came to town the next day and beat them 9–3.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/dodgers/news/shohei-ohtani-takes-on-field-batting-practice|title=Ohtani takes rare on-field batting practice, but slump continues -- for now|access-date=May 12, 2026|date=May 11, 2026|first=Sonja|last=Chen |website=[[MLB.com]] }}</ref> [[Eric Haase]] hit two home runs as the Dodgers lost, 6–2, in the next game. It was the first time since [[1936 Brooklyn Dodgers season|1936]], and only second time ever, that the Dodgers had lost four straight games by four or more runs.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/dodgers/news/yoshinobu-yamamoto-faces-giants-for-second-time-in-2026|title=Dodgers drop 4th straight by 4+ runs for first time since 1936|access-date=May 13, 2026|date=May 13, 2026|first=Sonja|last=Chen |website=[[MLB.com]] }}</ref> Ohtani struck out eight in seven scoreless innings the following day as the Dodgers ended the losing streak with a 4–0 win.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/shohei-ohtani-pitches-vs-giants?game_pk=823952|title=Ohtani's historic stretch, 0.82 ERA prompt clubhouse Cy Young chatter|access-date=May 14, 2026|date=May 13, 2026|first=Sonja|last=Chen |website=[[MLB.com]] }}</ref> In the series finale, [[Emmet Sheehan]] struck out six in six innings and [[Will Smith (catcher)|Will Smith]] homered as the Dodgers won 5–2.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/dodgers/news/will-smith-hits-leadoff-home-run-as-dodgers-beat-giants|title=No Sho, no problem: Smith's leadoff HR sparks Dodgers vs. SF|access-date=May 15, 2026|date=May 15, 2026|first=Courtney|last=Hollmon |website=[[MLB.com]] }}</ref>

On May 15, the Dodgers traveled to [[Angel Stadium]] to play the [[Los Angeles Angels]]. [[Andy Pages]] hit a three-run homer, followed by a solo homer by [[Max Muncy]] in the fourth inning and the Dodgers shut out the Angels, 6–0. With Snell going back on the injured list before the game, the Dodgers used eight different relief pitchers to get through the game.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/dodgers/news/blake-snell-scratched-from-start-against-angels|title=Dodgers place Snell on IL with loose bodies in elbow; surgery not ruled out|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=May 15, 2026|access-date=May 16, 2026}}</ref> The next day, the Dodgers scored 15 runs on 10 hits and two errors in a 15–2 rout of the Angels. Ohtani had five RBI while hitting a double and a triple.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/shohei-ohtani-hits-little-league-homer-in-dodgers-rout-of-angels?game_pk=824038|title=You've never seen an Ohtani 'homer' quite like this one|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=May 16, 2026|access-date=May 17, 2026}}</ref> [[Roki Sasaki]] struck out eight in seven innings while only giving up one run on four hits and the Dodgers finished a sweep of the Angels with a 10–1 win.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/roki-sasaki-win-vs-angels?game_pk=824034|title=No, that's not a typo: Sasaki goes 7 innings with ZERO walks|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=May 17, 2026|access-date=May 17, 2026}}</ref> The next series was at [[Petco Park]] against the [[San Diego Padres]]. [[Yoshinobu Yamamoto]] struck out eight while allowing only one run (a solo homer by the second batter of the game, [[Miguel Andújar]]) on three hits. However, the Dodgers were shut out by [[Michael King (baseball)|Michael King]] and two relievers and lost 1–0.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/dodgers/news/yoshinobu-yamamoto-starts-vs-padres|title=Yamamoto impressive vs. Padres, but 1st-inning HR continues unwanted trends|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=May 18, 2026|access-date=May 19, 2026}}</ref> [[Freddie Freeman]] had two home runs in the following game and as the Dodgers came from behind and beat the Padres 5–4 on a sacrifice fly by Pages in the eighth inning.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/andy-pages-dodgers-best-mason-miller-padres-for-late-win?game_pk=823302|title='One of the best at-bats I've ever seen': Pages wins epic battle vs. Miller as Dodgers top Padres|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=May 19, 2026|access-date=May 20, 2026}}</ref> In the series finale, Ohtani pitched five scoreless innings and also homered in a 4–0 shutout.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/shohei-ohtani-starts-vs-padres?game_pk=823299|title=Ohtani blasts leadoff HR, lowers ERA to 0.73 in return to hitting while pitching|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=May 20, 2026|access-date=May 21, 2026}}</ref> The Dodgers traveled on May 22 to play the [[Milwaukee Brewers]] at [[American Family Field]]. [[William Contreras]] hit a three-run home run in the first inning off Wrobleski and the Brewers won the opener, 5–1.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/dodgers/news/shohei-ohtani-drives-in-only-dodgers-run-in-loss-to-brewers|title=Regular season in Milwaukee remains a puzzle for Dodgers|work=mlb.com|first=Jackson|last=Stone|date=May 22, 2026|access-date=May 23, 2026}}</ref> The Dodgers got even with a 11–3 win the following day.[[Teoscar Hernández]] had three hits, including a home run, and drove in six runs.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/roki-sasaki-leads-dodgers-to-big-win-over-brewers?game_pk=823787|title=Sasaki displays newfound confidence in Dodgers' rout of Brewers|work=mlb.com|first=Jackson|last=Stone|date=May 23, 2026|access-date=May 24, 2026}}</ref> In that game, the Dodgers bullpen set a new franchise record for scoreless innings with 36 in a row, breaking the previous mark set in [[1998 Los Angeles Dodgers season|the 1998 season]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.truebluela.com/los-angeles-dodgers-history-records/116672/bullpen-scoreless-streak-record|title=Dodgers bullpen scoreless streak sets franchise record|work=SB Nation|first=Eric|last=Stephen|date=May 24, 2026|access-date=May 24, 2026}}</ref> The Dodgers finished off the series with a 7–1 win. Yamamoto allowed only the one run in seven innings while Pages homered in the game.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/yoshinobu-yamamoto-starts-against-brewers-in-series-finale?game_pk=823786|title=Yamamoto in 'complete control' in first Milwaukee start since NLCS gem|work=mlb.com|first=Jackson|last=Stone|date=May 24, 2026|access-date=May 24, 2026}}</ref>

The Dodgers returned home on [[Memorial Day]] to play the [[Colorado Rockies]]. [[Emmet Sheehan]] struck out eight batters in six innings and the Dodgers scored four runs in the seventh to win 5–3.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/dodgers-bullpen-s-scoreless-streak-ends-in-comeback-win?game_pk=823948|title='We'll start another one': Dodgers come back after 'pen's scoreless streak ends|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=May 25, 2026|access-date=May 26, 2026}}</ref> The next day, [[Eric Lauer]], recently acquired in a trade from the [[Toronto Blue Jays]], made his Dodgers debut and allowed one run in six innings as the starting pitcher. [[Mookie Betts]] had three hits with two home runs, Pages had two doubles and a homer and the Dodgers won 15–6.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/mookie-betts-moves-down-in-dodgers-lineup-to-cleanup?game_pk=823949|title=Cleanup? You Betts! Mookie homers twice in 1st game in 4-spot since 2017|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=May 27, 2026|access-date=May 27, 2026}}</ref> On May 27, Ohtani struck out seven and did not allow a hit in six innings and the Dodgers as a team only allowed one hit in the game as they beat the Rockies to finish off the sweep, 4–1.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/shohei-ohtani-starts-against-rockies?game_pk=823947|title=6 hitless innings, a leadoff HR and he's ... angry? Ohtani a 'crazy good competitor'|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=May 27, 2026|access-date=May 28, 2026}}</ref> The next series was against the [[Philadelphia Phillies]]. In the opener, Wrobleski struck out nine in seven innings and the Dodgers hit four solo home runs for a 4–2 win.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/justin-wrobleski-brilliant-in-start-vs-phillies?game_pk=823946|title=Wrobleski's velo ticks up in 7-inning gem with career-high 9 K's|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=May 29, 2026|access-date=May 30, 2026}}</ref> In the next game, Betts had three hits including a double and Sasaki struck out seven while only allowing one run on three hits in {{fraction|5|1|3}} innings. However, the Phillies scored three runs in the ninth, with a go-ahead two run homer by [[Edmundo Sosa]] giving them a 4–3 win.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/edmundo-sosa-hits-go-ahead-home-run-in-eighth-inning?game_pk=823945|title=Mattingly's decision to insert Sosa as pinch-hitter pays off ... two innings later|work=mlb.com|first=Paul|last=Casella|date=May 30, 2026|access-date=May 31, 2026}}</ref> The month ended with a 9–1 victory over the Phillies. Yamamoto struck out 10 in {{fraction|5|1|3}} scoreless innings while the Dodgers hit three home runs (including the first major league homer by [[Ryan Ward (baseball)|Ryan Ward]]).<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/yoshinobu-yamamoto-starts-series-finale-vs-phillies?game_pk=823944|title=Yamamoto racks up 10 K's, holds Phils scoreless -- and is hungry for more|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=May 31, 2026|access-date=June 1, 2026}}</ref>

===June===
The Dodgers started June with a road series against the [[Arizona Diamondbacks]] at [[Chase Field]]. The Diamondbacks scored four runs (on three home runs) from the sixth to eighth innings to win 4–1.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/d-backs-three-homers-beat-dodgers?game_pk=825078|title=D-backs get contributions across the board to top red-hot Dodgers|work=mlb.com|first=Jesse|last=Borek|date=June 1, 2026|access-date=June 2, 2026}}</ref> The Dodgers jumped out to a 4–0 lead the following day and then held on for a 6–5 win.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/dodgers-hold-off-d-backs-on-big-night-by-shohei-ohtani?game_pk=825079|title=Shohei's recent surge coincides with Dodgers' dominant stretch|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=June 2, 2026|access-date=June 3, 2026}}</ref> [[Shohei Ohtani]] pitched six scoreless innings and [[Kyle Tucker]] hit a two-run home run as the Dodgers shut out the Diamondbacks, 7–0, in the following game.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/shohei-ohtani-pitches-scoreless-gem-vs-d-backs?game_pk=825077|title=Ohtani lowers ERA to 0.74 with 6-inning gem -- and reaches base 5 times!|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=June 3, 2026|access-date=June 4, 2026}}</ref> [[Justin Wrobleski]] pitched six shutout innings but the Diamondbacks won the last game of the series, 3–2, thanks to a walk-off home run by [[Ketel Marte]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/ketel-marte-s-walk-off-home-run-lifts-d-backs-past-dodgers?game_pk=825076|title='A big-moment player': Marte's walk-off HR caps dramatic D-backs rally|work=mlb.com|first=Steve|last=Gilbert|date=June 4, 2026|access-date=June 6, 2026}}</ref>

The Dodgers returned home on June 5 for a weekend series against the [[Los Angeles Angels]]. [[Roki Sasaki]] struck out 10 while only allowing two hits in seven shutout innings while a [[Freddie Freeman]] walk-off home run games the Dodgers the 1–0 win in the series opener.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/roki-sasaki-strikes-out-10-freddie-freeman-hits-walk-off-homer?game_pk=823943|title=Sasaki (10 K's, 7 scoreless) looking more like pitcher LA 'hoped to get'|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=June 5, 2026|access-date=June 6, 2026}}</ref> In the second game, the Dodgers won 9–2, with all nine runs scored in the first inning.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/dodgers-score-9-runs-in-first-inning-vs-angels?game_pk=823942|title=9 runs! Ohtani's speed, power bookend Dodgers' biggest 1st inning in 5 years|work=mlb.com|first=Courtney|last=Hollmon|date=June 6, 2026|access-date=June 7, 2026}}</ref> In the final game of the series, [[Sebastián Rivero]] had five hits in five at bats with six RBI as the Angels won, 13–5.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/sebastian-rivero-jo-adell-star-in-angels-big-win-vs-dodgers?game_pk=823941|title=Angels' bottom four hitters romp in big win over Dodgers|work=mlb.com|first=Rhett|last=Bollinger|date=June 7, 2026|access-date=June 7, 2026}}</ref>

The next road trip began on June 9, against the [[Pittsburgh Pirates]] at [[PNC Park]]. They scored 10 runs in the seventh inning to win 12–2.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/dodgers-10-run-inning-against-pirates?game_pk=823374|title=Dodgers celebrate Skenes' exit with a 10-run seventh|work=mlb.com|first=John|last=Perrotto|date=June 9, 2026|access-date=June 10, 2026}}</ref> In the next game, [[Ryan Ward (baseball)|Ryan Ward]] hit his first career grand slam home run to give the Dodgers the lead, but [[Tyler Callihan]] hit two home runs as the Pirates came from behind to win 9–8.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/dodgers/news/shohei-ohtani-starts-for-dodgers-vs-pirates|title='This one stung': Ohtani's HR can't save LA after he allows season high in runs|work=mlb.com|first=John|last=Perrotto|date=June 10, 2026|access-date=June 11, 2026}}</ref> The Dodgers won 8–6 in the series finale.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/shohei-ohtani-left-knee-inflammation?game_pk=823371|title=Dodgers optimistic after Ohtani exits finale with left knee inflammation|work=mlb.com|first=John|last=Perrotto|date=June 11, 2026|access-date=June 12, 2026}}</ref> The road trip moved to [[Rate Field]] for a series against the [[Chicago White Sox]]. [[Roki Sasaki]] gave up seven runs in the fifth as the Dodgers dropped the series opener, 8–2.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/miguel-vargas-chase-meidroth-lead-white-sox-to-win-vs-dodgers?game_pk=824588|title=First-place White Sox keep rolling in playoff-like atmosphere at The Rate|work=mlb.com|first=Scott|last=Merkin|date=June 12, 2026|access-date=June 13, 2026}}</ref> On June 13, [[Yoshinobu Yamamoto]] took a [[Perfect game (baseball)|perfect game]] into the eighth inning and a [[no-hitter]] into the ninth before allowing a solo home run to [[Tristan Peters]] to lead off the inning. Ohtani hit a lead-off home run and [[Max Muncy]] added two as the Dodgers won, 7–1.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/yoshinobu-yamamoto-loses-perfect-game-in-eighth-no-hitter-in-ninth?game_pk=824587|title=Yamamoto comes THIS close to perfection -- then a no-no -- vs. White Sox|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=June 13, 2026|access-date=June 14, 2026}}</ref> [[Emmet Sheehan]] struck out eight while allowing only on hit through the first five innings of the series finale. However, [[Sam Antonacci]] led off the sixth with a solo home run and then the White Sox hit two home runs off reliver [[Jack Dreyer]], part of six runs in the inning to win the game, 6–4.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/white-sox-comeback-with-big-6th-inning-to-beat-dodgers?game_pk=824586|title=White Sox erupt in 6th inning in comeback win over Dodgers|work=mlb.com|first=Scott|last=Merkin|date=June 14, 2026|access-date=June 14, 2026}}</ref>

The next home series began with three-games against the [[Tampa Bay Rays]]. The Rays opened the game with a two-run first inning home run by [[Ryan Vilade]] and took a 3–0 lead in the second only for the Dodgers to tie it with a three-run [[Kyle Tucker]] homer in the bottom of the inning. A pinch-hit solo homer by [[Miguel Rojas (baseball)|Miguel Rojas]] in the seventh broke the tie and gave the Dodgers a 4–3 win.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/miguel-rojas-pinch-hit-home-run-lifts-dodgers-over-rays?game_pk=823938|title=Rojas jumps on first pitch for second pinch-hit homer of career|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=June 15, 2026|access-date=June 16, 2026}}</ref> In the next game, a solo homerun by Ohtani in the sixth inning accounted for all the scoring in a 1–0 Dodgers win.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/shohei-ohtani-homers-for-only-run-in-dodgers-win-vs-rays?game_pk=823939|title=Shohei's solo shot proves to be the difference in pitchers' duel|work=mlb.com|first=Courtney|last=Hollmon|date=June 16, 2026|access-date=June 17, 2026}}</ref> The Dodgers finished off the series sweep with a 5–4 win. Freeman's two-run sixth inning home run was the difference.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/shohei-ohtani-allows-season-high-four-runs-vs-rays?game_pk=823940|title=Ohtani's unusual start on mound ends with surprise hitting opportunity|work=mlb.com|first=Courtney|last=Hollmon|date=June 17, 2026|access-date=June 18, 2026}}</ref> Next up was the [[Baltimore Orioles]]. The Dodgers took an early 3–0 lead, fell behind 5–3 after the Orioles scored all their runs in the sixth and seventh innings, and then won 6–5 on a solo homer by [[Mookie Betts]] and a two-run walk-off hit by [[Dalton Rushing]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/dalton-rushing-hits-first-career-walk-off?game_pk=823936|title=0-for-4 with 3 K's? 'None of it matters' after Rushing knocks 1st career walk-off hit|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=June 20, 2026|access-date=June 20, 2026}}</ref> The Orioles won the next game, 3–2, as the Dodgers ninth inning rally fell short.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/dodgers/news/yoshinobu-yamamoto-faces-baltimore-orioles|title=Yamamoto struggles to find 'feel' as Dodgers' comeback falls short|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=June 20, 2026|access-date=June 21, 2026}}</ref> The Orioles blew out the Dodgers on June 21, 12–1, to take the series.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/dodgers/news/dodgers-lose-series-to-orioles-allow-12-runs-in-finale|title=After humbling set vs. O's, Dodgers believe their best baseball lies ahead|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=June 21, 2026|access-date=June 22, 2026}}</ref>

The Dodgers traveled to [[Target Field]] to play the [[Minnesota Twins]] and took the opener of that series, 2–1. Solo home runs by Ohtani and Freeman accounted for both of the runs.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/will-klein-opens-for-eric-lauer-vs-twins?game_pk=823692|title=After humbling set vs. O's, Dodgers believe their best baseball lies ahead|work=mlb.com|first=Courtney|last=Hollmon|date=June 22, 2026|access-date=June 23, 2026}}</ref> The following game, Wrobleski allowed two runs in seven innings while the Dodgers offense came alive and they won 12–3.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/justin-wrobleski-pitches-dodgers-to-win-over-twins?game_pk=823690|title=Wrobleski bolsters All-Star case with 7 strong innings in Dodgers rout|work=mlb.com|first=Courtney|last=Hollmon|date=June 23, 2026|access-date=June 24, 2026}}</ref> The Dodgers finished off a sweep of the series with a 4–3 win on June 24. Ohtani struck out sight in six innings and Betts hit his 300th career home run.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/shohei-ohtani-pitches-against-twins?game_pk=823691|title=Ohtani gets bounce-back he needed in strong start for series sweep|work=mlb.com|first=Courtney|last=Hollmon|date=June 24, 2026|access-date=June 24, 2026}}</ref> After an off-day, they began a weekend series against the [[San Diego Padres]] at [[Petco Park]] with a 7–1 defeat.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/dodgers/news/roki-sasaki-walks-five-as-dodgers-lose-to-padres|title=Command, mechanical issues still limiting Sasaki on the hill
|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=June 26, 2026|access-date=June 27, 2026}}</ref> In the next game, [[Kyle Tucker]] had three hits, including a home run, and four RBI as the Dodgers scored nine runs in the sixth enroute to a 15–3 win.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/dodgers-score-9-runs-in-6th-inning-in-win-vs-padres?game_pk=823283|title=Dodgers score 9 in 6th for their biggest inning in San Diego in nearly 6 decades
|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=June 27, 2026|access-date=June 28, 2026}}</ref> The Dodgers took the series finale, 4–2, with Betts driving in two runs on a hit in the fifth.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/mookie-betts-stays-hot-as-dodgers-beat-padres?game_pk=823281|title=Betts continues red-hot June as Dodgers take series over Padres
|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=June 28, 2026|access-date=June 28, 2026}}</ref> Next up was a series at [[Sutter Health Park]] against the [[Athletics (baseball)|Athletics]]. The Dodgers recorded 17 hits, three of which were home runs, in a 9–4 win.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/dodgers-hit-3-homers-to-beat-athletics?game_pk=824986|title=Dodgers take advantage of hitters' park, smash 3 home runs vs. A's
|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=June 29, 2026|access-date=June 30, 2026}}</ref> the next day, Wrobleski struck out 11 batters in seven innings as the Dodgers won 9–3. It was the 1,000th career win for manager [[Dave Roberts (baseball manager)|Dave Roberts]], the fastest to reach that mark in MLB history.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/dave-roberts-earns-1-000th-managerial-win-as-dodgers-top-a-s?game_pk=824984|title='It's mind-blowing': Roberts becomes fastest MLB manager to 1,000 career wins
|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=June 30, 2026|access-date=July 1, 2026}}</ref>

===July===
[[File:P20260723JB-1116.jpg|thumb|The Los Angeles Dodgers visiting the [[White House]] on July 23, 2026.]]
The Dodgers began July by being blown out by the Athletics, 7–1.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/shea-langeliers-homers-in-a-s-win-over-dodgers?game_pk=824985|title=Langeliers bolsters All-Star case with 20th homer
|work=mlb.com|first=Martin|last=Gallegos|date=July 1, 2026|access-date=July 2, 2026}}</ref>

They returned home the next day for a four-game series with the [[San Diego Padres]]. The series started with the Padres scoring six runs in the first two innings to take a big lead, only for the Dodgers to come from behind and win, 12–7. [[Kyle Tucker]] had four hits in four at-bats and scored three runs, and [[Dalton Rushing]] also had four hits, including a home run and a double.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/dodgers-turn-early-deficit-into-big-win-over-padres?game_pk=823935|title=Never-say-die Dodgers turn 6-run deficit into 5-run victory over Padres
|work=mlb.com|first=Courtney|last=Hollmon|date=July 2, 2026|access-date=July 3, 2026}}</ref> A grand slam home run by [[Teoscar Hernández]] in the seventh gave the Dodgers a 4–3 win the next day.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/teoscar-hernandez-hits-go-ahead-grand-slam-in-dodgers-win?game_pk=823933|title='We never give up': Teo's grand slam keys LA's 2nd straight comeback win
|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=July 2, 2026|access-date=July 3, 2026}}</ref> [[Yoshinobu Yamamoto]] pitched on [[Independence Day (United States)|the Fourth of July]], striking out 10 while allowing only three hits in seven shutout innings homered in a 3–0 win.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/yoshinobu-yamamoto-starts-vs-padres-for-third-time-in-2026?game_pk=823932|title=Yamamoto earns ASG nod, then quiets Padres (yet again) with 10 K's in 7 scoreless
|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=July 4, 2026|access-date=July 5, 2026}}</ref> The Padres managed to prevent a sweep with a 5–2 win in the series finale. [[Manny Machado]] hit a three-run home run in the seventh.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/manny-machado-s-homer-lifts-padres-past-dodgers-to-snap-8-game-skid?game_pk=823931|title=Machado's clutch blast snaps Padres' 8-game skid
|work=mlb.com|first=AJ|last=Cassavell|date=July 5, 2026|access-date=July 6, 2026}}</ref> A series with the [[Colorado Rockies]] at Dodger Stadium was up next. In the first game, Rushing had three hits, including two doubles, and the walk-off game-winning hit in the 11th inning.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/dalton-rushing-has-walk-off-hit-in-dodgers-first-extra-inning-game?game_pk=823930|title=Rushing the walk-off hero in Dodgers' 1st extra-inning game -- and 60th win!
|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=July 6, 2026|access-date=July 7, 2026}}</ref> This was the Dodgers' first game of the season to go into [[extra innings]], making Los Angeles' 92-game streak of games without extra innings the second longest in MLB history, with the [[2005 Boston Red Sox season|2005 Boston Red Sox]] needing 99 games to finally play an extra inning contest.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Pagaduan |first=Jedd |date=2026-07-07 |title=Unusual 92-game Dodgers streak ends in thrilling series opener vs. Rockies |url=https://clutchpoints.com/mlb/los-angeles-dodgers/dodgers-news-unusual-92-game-streak-ends-in-thrilling-series-opener-vs-rockies |access-date=2026-07-07 |website=ClutchPoints}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Heyen |first=Billy |date=2026-07-07 |title=Dodgers needed 92 games to finally do something that's pretty normal in MLB |url=https://www.msn.com/en-us/sports/mlb/dodgers-needed-92-games-to-finally-do-something-that-s-pretty-normal-in-mlb/ar-AA27ph7v?uxmode=ruby&ctsrc=dgst&ocid=edgntpruby&pc=ASTS&cvid=6a4d3241945f4c339864ae296e20180f&ei=7 |access-date=2026-07-07 |website=www.msn.com}}</ref> [[Shohei Ohtani]] led of the next game with his 300th career home run but the Rockies came from behind, scoring three runs in the eighth, to win 4–3.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/dodgers/news/shohei-ohtani-hits-300th-career-home-run|title=Shohei hits another milestone with 300th HR, but there are loftier goals in mind|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen |date=July 7, 2026|access-date=July 8, 2026}}</ref> The Dodgers took the final game in the series, 4–3.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/mookie-betts-gets-game-winning-hit-against-rockies?game_pk=823928|title=Roki hangs tough before Betts breaks through late for LA|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen |date=July 8, 2026|access-date=July 9, 2026}}</ref> The Dodgers next began a three-game series with the [[Arizona Diamondbacks]] with a bullpen game. [[Andy Pages]] had three hits and a home run but the Dodgers committed three errors and lost 9–3.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/eduardo-rodriguez-earns-first-win-against-dodgers-this-season?game_pk=823927|title=Rodriguez gives up back-to-back homers in first, then shuts down Dodgers|work=mlb.com|first=Kyle|last=Glasser|date=July 10, 2026|access-date=July 11, 2026}}</ref> The next day, the Diamondbacks scored five runs in the fifth inning enroute to a 9–2 win.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/dodgers/news/yoshinobu-yamamoto-makes-final-start-before-all-star-break|title=Yamamoto falters in final start of stellar 1st half; won't pitch in ASG|work=mlb.com|first=Jacob and Sonja Chen|last=Gurvis|date=July 11, 2026|access-date=July 12, 2026}}</ref> The Dodgers lost again to the Diamondbacks, 5–3, on July 12 to head into the all-star break by being swept for the first time in the 2026 season.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/d-backs-end-first-half-with-sweep-of-dodgers?game_pk=823925|title=D-backs end 1st half with sweep of Dodgers as NL Wild Card race heats up|work=mlb.com|first=Kyle|last=Glaser|date=July 12, 2026|access-date=July 12, 2026}}</ref>

The Dodgers had six players selected to the [[2026 Major League Baseball All-Star Game]], which was played on July 14 at [[Citizens Bank Park]] in [[Philadelphia]]. Ohtani was the top vote-getter in the league, while [[Freddie Freeman]], [[Max Muncy]] and Pages were also voted as starters, and Yamamoto and [[Justin Wrobleski]] were voted in as reserves.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/2026-mlb-all-star-game-team-by-team-breakdown|title=Here's a team-by-team breakdown from 2026 All-Star Game|work=mlb.com|date=July 14, 2026|access-date=July 15, 2026}}</ref>

After the All-Star break, the Dodgers opened up the second half of the season at [[Yankee Stadium]] for a series with the [[New York Yankees]]. [[Roki Sasaki]] struck out five in {{fraction|5|2|3}} innings, and a two-run home run by Muncy in the seventh inning gave the Dodgers a 2–1 win.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/roki-sasaki-max-muncy-lead-dodgers-over-yankees?game_pk=823524|title=Best version of Sasaki helps Dodgers get back on feet to open half|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=July 17, 2026|access-date=July 18, 2026}}</ref> The Dodgers had to play a doubleheader on July 19 after a rainout the previous day. In the opener, Yamamoto pitched a complete game and the Dodgers scored five runs in the eighth to win 8–2.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/dodgers/news/yoshinobu-yamamoto-throws-complete-game-against-yankees|title='Why he's our ace': Yamamoto twirls 1st regular-season CG with another Bronx gem|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=July 19, 2026|access-date=July 19, 2026}}</ref> The Yankees won the last game of the series, 2–1, with an eighth inning [[Jazz Chisholm Jr.]] home run the deciding factor.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/dodgers/news/tommy-edman-hits-tying-homer-before-dodgers-lose-to-yankees|title=Despite Edman's pinch blast, Dodgers settle for DH split to end long day in Bronx|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=July 19, 2026|access-date=July 20, 2026}}</ref> Next up was a series with the [[Philadelphia Phillies]] at Citizens Bank Park. [[Mookie Betts]] hit two home runs in the opener, but the Dodgers lost 10–7.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/dodgers/news/mookie-betts-hits-two-homers-on-four-hit-night|title=Dropped to 5th in order, Betts smashes 2 HRs on 4-hit night|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=July 20, 2026|access-date=July 21, 2026}}</ref> The next game was a pitching duel between Wrobleski and [[Zach Wheeler]] of the Phillies. Wrobleski allowed one run in {{fraction|6|1|3}} innings while Wheeler struck out nine in seven innings. The Dodgers won 2–1 on a two-run home run by Muncy in the fourth inning.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/max-muncy-ties-for-fifth-most-homers-in-dodgers-history?game_pk=823437|title=Muncy's winning HR -- off Wheeler! -- ties Penguin for 5th on Dodgers' list|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=July 21, 2026|access-date=July 22, 2026}}</ref> The Dodgers won the next game, 9–5, as Muncy and [[Dalton Rushing]] homered.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/dalton-rushing-leads-dodgers-to-series-win-over-phillies?game_pk=823438|title=Rushing (HR, 3 RBIs) making most of everyday catcher's role in Smith's absence|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=July 22, 2026|access-date=July 23, 2026}}</ref> After an off-day, they began a series against the [[New York Mets]] at [[Citi Field]]. Sasaki struck out nine in seven innings while only allowing three hits and Kyle Tucker homered in a 4–2 win.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/roki-sasaki-strikes-out-9-in-7-innings-vs-mets?game_pk=823601|title=Sasaki keeps Mets guessing with nearly unhittable splitter|work=mlb.com|first=Bill|last=Ladson|date=July 24, 2026|access-date=July 25, 2026}}</ref> The next day, Yamamoto allowed one run in six innings and Tucker homered again in a 4–3 win.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/yoshinobu-yamamoto-throws-quality-start-vs-mets?game_pk=823600|title=Yamamoto's Big Apple success continues vs. Mets with 15th quality start|work=mlb.com|first=Bill|last=Ladson|date=July 25, 2026|access-date=July 26, 2026}}</ref> The Mets came from behind in the next game, scoring seven runs between the sixth and seven innings to win 8–3.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/tyrone-taylor-s-three-run-homer-sends-mets-past-dodgers?game_pk=823599|title=Mets' trade chips on display in win over Dodgers|work=mlb.com|first=Anthony|last=Dicomo|date=July 26, 2026|access-date=July 27, 2026}}</ref>

The Dodgers returned home on July 28 to play the [[Seattle Mariners]]. Ohtani and Muncy homered in the opener but the Mariners came from behind to win, 7–6, thanks to two home runs by [[Dominic Canzone]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/mariners-hit-season-high-5-homers-in-win-over-dodgers?game_pk=823923|title=Canzone (2 HRs) leads Mariners' 5-HR night to hold off Dodgers|work=mlb.com|first=Daniel|last=Kramer|date=July 28, 2026|access-date=July 29, 2026}}</ref> The Dodgers took the next game, 4–2, as [[Eric Lauer]] allowed only one hit in six scoreless innings and Pages homered.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/edwin-diaz-activated-from-injured-list?game_pk=823924|title=Cue the trumpets! Díaz returns on his bobblehead night, earns save|work=mlb.com|first=Courtney|last=Hollmon|date=July 29, 2026|access-date=July 30, 2026}}</ref> Sasaki struck out seven in {{fraction|5|1|3}} innings and Rushing hit a three-run home run as the Dodgers took the series finale, 6–2.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/roki-sasaki-strikes-out-seven-earns-win-vs-mariners?game_pk=823921|title=Sasaki shows encouraging command of signature splitter, until wobbly 6th inning|work=mlb.com|first=Sarah|last=Wexler|date=July 31, 2026|access-date=July 31, 2026}}</ref> They ended the month by welcoming the [[Boston Red Sox]] to town. The Red Sox scored five runs off the Dodgers bullpen in the seventh inning to come from behind and win 9–4.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/red-sox-continue-strong-west-coast-road-trip-in-win-over-dodgers?game_pk=823922|title=Does July have to end? Red Sox cap most successful month in franchise history|work=mlb.com|first=Doug|last=Padilla|date=July 31, 2026|access-date=August 1, 2026}}</ref>

===August===
[[Yoshinobu Yamamoto]] began August by allowing three runs (two of them solo home runs by [[Ceddanne Rafaela]]) in eight innings as the Dodgers lost 3–2 to the Red Sox.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/ceddanne-rafaela-hits-two-home-runs-in-red-sox-win-over-dodgers?game_pk=823920|title=Rafaela's 2-HR night extends Red Sox surge|work=mlb.com|first=Courtney|last=Hollmon|date=August 1, 2026|access-date=August 2, 2026}}</ref> The Red Sox also won the next day, 8–4, to finish off a sweep of the Dodgers for the first time since the [[2010 Los Angeles Dodgers season|2010 season]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/red-sox-sweep-dodgers-for-first-time-since-2010?game_pk=823919|title=Scorching Sox sweep Dodgers for first time since 2010|work=mlb.com|first=Courtney|last=Hollmon|date=August 2, 2026|access-date=August 3, 2026}}</ref>

The Dodgers traveled to [[Wrigley Field]] to play the [[Chicago Cubs]]. The Dodgers lost 10–5.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/cubs-trades-kevin-gausman-clay-holmes?game_pk=824647|title=In Gausman, Holmes trades, Cubs reward rotation for grinding through '26|work=mlb.com|first=Jordan|last=Bastian|date=August 3, 2026|access-date=August 4, 2026}}</ref> The Dodgers started [[Tarik Skubal]] on August 4, after acquiring him at the trade deadline from the [[Detroit Tigers]]. He allowed two runs in six innings while striking out six, but that was enough to cost the Dodgers the game as the offense remained punchless and the bullpen again faltered. The Dodgers lost their fifth straight game, for the first time all season, 5–1.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/javier-assad-cubs-lineup-delivers-against-dodgers?game_pk=824645|title=Cubs spoil Skubal's Dodgers debut in raucous night at Wrigley|work=mlb.com|first=Jordan|last=Bastian|date=August 4, 2026|access-date=August 5, 2026}}</ref> The Cubs finished off the sweep of the Dodgers with a 7–6 win in the series finale, despite [[Shohei Ohtani]] hitting two home runs in the game.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/shohei-ohtani-pete-crow-armstrong-trade-leadoff-homers?game_pk=824646|title=Shohei's leadoff HR pads MVP case, then PCA says 'not so fast'|work=mlb.com|first=Jordan|last=Bastian|date=August 5, 2026|access-date=August 6, 2026}}</ref> The Dodgers next traveled to [[Chase Field]] to play the [[Arizona Diamondbacks]] but started it off with their seventh straight loss, 4–3, on a two-run walk off home run by [[Ryan Waldschmidt]] off closer [[Edwin Díaz]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/dodgers/news/dodgers-losing-streak-reaches-7-games|title=Dodgers' skid reaches 7 games as Díaz struggles with slider|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=August 7, 2026|access-date=August 8, 2026}}</ref> Díaz blew another save the next day, but the Dodgers managed to win, 2–1, in 10 innings, to snap the losing streak.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/dodgers-losing-streak-ends-after-7-games?game_pk=825049|title=After late-inning drama, Dodgers claw back for 1st win in a week|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=August 8, 2026|access-date=August 9, 2026}}</ref> However, the Diamondbacks finished the series off with a 4–2 win.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/dodgers/news/dodgers-lose-2026-season-series-vs-diamondbacks|title=Dodgers drop season series vs. D-backs. What does that mean for NL West race?|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=August 9, 2026|access-date=August 9, 2026}}</ref>

The Dodgers returned home on August 10 to play the [[Kansas City Royals]]. The Dodgers came from behind to win 6–5.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/max-muncy-freddie-freeman-give-dodgers-clutch-hits-to-beat-royals?game_pk=823918|title=Feeling urgency after tough road trip, Dodgers conjure some clutch magic|work=mlb.com|first=Courtney|last=Hollmon|date=August 10, 2026|access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref> The Dodgers won again the next day, 5–4, on a walk-off hit by [[Max Muncy]] in the 10th inning.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/max-muncy-walks-off-royals-in-10th-inning?game_pk=823917|title=Muncy delivers AGAIN with bases loaded -- this time with walk-off in 10th|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=August 11, 2026|access-date=August 12, 2026}}</ref> They finished off the sweep of the Royals with a 4–2 win. Ohtani and [[Mookie Betts]] each had two hits, including a home run and [[Eric Lauer]] struck out six in {{fraction|6|1|3}} innings.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/dodgers-sweep-royals-to-remain-tied-for-2nd-best-nl-record?game_pk=823916|title=Dodgers reset with sweep of Royals ahead of crucial set vs. Brewers|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=August 12, 2026|access-date=August 13, 2026}}</ref> Next up were the [[Milwaukee Brewers]]. The Dodgers took a 4–2 lead after Muncy hit a three-run home run in the sixth inning, only for Díaz to blow another save and the Brewers won the game, 5–4.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/dodgers/news/edwin-diaz-blows-third-save-in-last-four-outings|title=Díaz blows third save in a week, and LA misses chance to tie for NL's best record|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=August 14, 2026|access-date=August 14, 2026}}</ref> Yamamoto struck out nine while allowing only one run in six innings the following day while [[Andy Pages]] and [[Tommy Edman]] homered in a 3–1 win.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/yoshinobu-yamamoto-throws-6-innings-of-1-run-ball-vs-brewers?game_pk=823913|title=Yamamoto dominates through 6, then Díaz gets redemption with a save|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=August 14, 2026|access-date=August 15, 2026}}</ref> [[Justin Wrobleski]] struck out six in six innings the next day, but allowed two two-run home runs as the Dodgers lost 4–1.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/dodgers/news/justin-wrobleski-allows-two-homers-in-loss-to-brewers|title=Wrobleski aims to get back to All-Star form as second-half struggles continue|work=mlb.com|first=Doug|last=Padilla|date=August 15, 2026|access-date=August 16, 2026}}</ref> The Brewers finished off the series with a 6–2 win.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/dodgers/news/tarik-skubal-strikes-out-7-vs-brewers|title=Skubal finishes with a flourish, but Dodgers lose potentially pivotal season series|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=August 16, 2026|access-date=August 17, 2026}}</ref>

The Dodgers went back on the road on August 17 for a quick three game road trip at [[Coors Field]] and Ohtani hit two home runs in a 11–5 win against the [[Colorado Rockies]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/shohei-ohtani-homer-caught-by-dodgers-reliever?game_pk=824320|title=Ohtani hits 878 feet of HRs ... and eyes possible bullpen session Tuesday|work=mlb.com|first=Patrick|last=Saunders|date=August 17, 2026|access-date=August 18, 2026}}</ref> Ohtani hit another home run, his 30th of the season, in the next game, a 7–6 win.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/shohei-ohtani-hits-30th-home-run-of-season-vs-rockies?game_pk=824319|title=Ohtani secures 6th straight 30-HR season to keep pace with PCA in tight NL MVP race|work=mlb.com|first=Patrick|last=Saunders|date=August 18, 2026|access-date=August 19, 2026}}</ref> The Dodgers swept the Rockies with a 6–4 10 inning victory.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/roki-sasaki-continues-2nd-half-dominance-in-dodgers-win-over-rockies?game_pk=824318|title=As LA's star-studded rotation nears full health, Sasaki shows why he belongs|work=mlb.com|first=Patrick|last=Saunders|date=August 19, 2026|access-date=August 20, 2026}}</ref>

They returned home to face the [[Pittsburgh Pirates]]. The Dodgers took an early lead when Muncy hit a two-run home run in the first and a two-run single by Betts in the third but the Pirates came back and tied the game with a two-run home run by [[Oneil Cruz]] in the eighth. The Dodgers won the game with a walk-off hit by Tommy Edman in the 10th.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/yoshinobu-yamamoto-has-quality-start-in-opener-vs-pirates?game_pk=823911|title=Edman's walk off the cherry on top after Yamamoto serves up another quality start|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=August 21, 2026|access-date=August 22, 2026}}</ref> In the following game, Skubal struck out 11 batters in seven innings and the Dodgers won 4–3.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/tarik-skubal-11-strikeouts-seven-innings-dodgers-pirates?game_pk=823910|title='That's the guy that we needed': Skubal (11 K's) shows Dodgers what he can do|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=August 22, 2026|access-date=August 23, 2026}}</ref> The Dodgers finished off the series with a 4–0 shutout win. [[Blake Snell]] struck out six while only allowing two hits in six shutout innings.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/dodgers-sweep-pirates-for-6th-straight-win?game_pk=823909|title=Dodgers match season-high win streak with 6th straight as they sweep Bucs|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=August 23, 2026|access-date=August 23, 2026}}</ref>

Back on the road on August 25 against the [[Atlanta Braves]] at [[Truist Park]], [[Tyler Glasnow]] returned from a lengthy injury absence and struck out seven in five innings. [[Ozzie Albies]] drove in the winning run in the eighth with an RBI single as the Braves won, 4–3.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/braves-rally-late-to-beat-dodgers-in-battle-for-top-2-nl-seeds?game_pk=824881|title=Albies' clutch single gives Braves big win over NL rival Dodgers|work=mlb.com|first=Mark|last=Bowman|date=August 25, 2026|access-date=August 26, 2026}}</ref> A walk-off hit by [[Ha-seong Kim]] gave the Braves a 6–5 win in the second game<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/ha-seong-kim-aj-smith-shawver-key-win-vs-dodgers?game_pk=824878|title=Unlikely hero Kim shows why Braves stuck by him with walk-off hit|work=mlb.com|first=Mark|last=Bowman|date=August 26, 2026|access-date=August 28, 2026}}</ref> and the Braves completed the sweep, 1–0, thanks to [[Chris Sale]] striking out 11 in a complete game shut out.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/chris-sale-throws-complete-game-shutout-to-sweep-dodgers?game_pk=824879|title=Sale K's 11 to record his first shutout since 2019, wrap up sweep of Dodgers|work=mlb.com|first=Mark|last=Bowman|date=August 27, 2026|access-date=August 28, 2026}}</ref> They moved on to [[Comerica Park]] for a series with the Tigers and Skubal greeted his former team by striking out seven and only allowing one run in six innings. An RBI single by [[Freddie Freeman]] in the eighth inning gave the Dodger a 2–1 win.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/tarik-skubal-returns-to-detroit-to-face-tigers?game_pk=824231|title=Skubal has emotional return to Detroit -- but dominates Tigers anyway|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=August 28, 2026|access-date=August 29, 2026}}</ref> The next day, Snell struck out 10 in five innings but the Tigers won 2–1 on a walk-off hit by [[Kevin McGonigle]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/javier-baez-kevin-mcgonigle-lead-tigers-to-walk-off-win?game_pk=824230|title=Hiss, catch, double: El Mago works his magic in wild 9th|work=mlb.com|first=Jason|last=Beck|date=August 29, 2026|access-date=August 30, 2026}}</ref> The series wrapped up with a 6–1 Dodgers win. Glasnow struck out six while allowing only two hits in seven innings and [[Teoscar Hernández]] hit a two-run homerun.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/teoscar-hernandez-tyler-glasnow-dodgers-tigers?game_pk=824232|title=Teoscar brings the thunder, Glasnow deals as Dodgers take series|work=mlb.com|first=Sonya|last=Chen|date=August 30, 2026|access-date=August 30, 2026}}</ref>

===September===
The Dodgers began September with a home series against the [[St. Louis Cardinals]] and lost the opener 13–8. They allowed five home runs, including two by [[Thomas Saggese]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/numbers-behind-cardinals-all-time-offensive-game-at-dodger-stadium?game_pk=823908|title=4 numbers that tell the story of Cards' wild, historic night in LA|work=mlb.com|first=Jacob|last=Gurvis|date=September 2, 2026|access-date=September 2, 2026}}</ref> In the next game, the Dodgers jumped out to an early lead only for the Cardinals to tie it up and then win the game 8–6, in 10 innings.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/thomas-saggese-leads-cardinals-in-extra-innings-win-over-dodgers?game_pk=823906|title=Relentless Cards outlast Yamamoto, claim extra-innings win to get back to .500|work=mlb.com|first=Jacob|last=Gurvis|date=September 2, 2026|access-date=September 3, 2026}}</ref> [[Tommy Edman]] had four hits and [[Teoscar Hernández]] drove in two runs in the ninth in the Dodgers 3–2 walkoff win to prevent the sweep.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/teoscar-hernandez-hits-walk-off-double-to-beat-cardinals?game_pk=823907|title=Teoscar, getting hot at just the right time, walks off Cardinals|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=September 4, 2026|access-date=September 4, 2026}}</ref> The Dodgers welcomed the [[Washington Nationals]] to town for a three-game series starting on September 4. [[Blake Snell]] struck out seven in six shutout innings as the Dodgers won 5–3.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/kyle-tucker-hits-home-run-in-dodgers-win-vs-nationals?game_pk=823905|title=Tucker homers at home for first time since June in Dodgers' win|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=September 4, 2026|access-date=September 5, 2026}}</ref> Edman's three-run home run in the seventh inning put the Dodgers ahead in a game they won 6–5.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/tommy-edman-hits-go-ahead-home-run-in-dodgers-win?game_pk=823904|title=No Ohtani, no problem, as Edman's 3-run blast lifts Dodgers over Nats|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=September 6, 2026|access-date=September 6, 2026}}</ref> The Dodgers finished the series off with a 7–5 win and a sweep of the Nationals. [[Mookie Betts]]' three-run home run in the eighth gave them the lead.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/mookie-betts-go-ahead-home-run-lifts-dodgers-over-nationals?game_pk=823903|title=Betts' homer, Tucker's robbery highlight key 8th inning to seal Dodgers' sweep|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=September 6, 2026|access-date=September 7, 2026}}</ref> In the next game, against the [[Cincinnati Reds]] on [[Labor Day (United States)|Labor Day]], [[Emmet Sheehan]] struck out 10 while allowing only three hits and one run in {{fraction|5|2|3}} innings while Teoscar Hernández hit a three-run home run in a 6–3 win.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/emmet-sheehan-strikes-out-10-as-dodgers-win-5th-straight?game_pk=823902|title=Sheehan (10 K's) earns spot in Dodgers' rotation as LA wins 5th straight
|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=September 7, 2026|access-date=September 8, 2026}}</ref> In the following game, [[Tarik Skubal]] pitched {{fraction|7|1|3}} innings, struck out five and allowed four hits (one of which was a two-run home run by [[Elly De La Cruz]]). The Dodgers won 3–2.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/tarik-skubal-starts-for-dodgers-vs-reds-on-sept-8?game_pk=823901|title=Skubal's longest Dodger start fuels LA's season-high-tying 6th straight win
|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=September 8, 2026|access-date=September 9, 2026}}</ref> The Dodgers finished off a sweep of the Reds with a 14–1 victory. [[Yoshinobu Yamamoto]] struck out 10 while allowing only three hits in seven innings and the Dodgers hit four home runs.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/yoshinobu-yamamoto-starts-finale-against-reds?game_pk=823900|title=Yamamoto (10 K's) dominates, Dodgers score 14 to run season-high win streak to 7
|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=September 10, 2026|access-date=September 10, 2026}}</ref>

The Dodgers began a road trip on September 11 against the [[Miami Marlins]] at [[loanDepot park]]. Snell struck out eight in {{fraction|5|2|3}} innings, [[Kyle Tucker]] hit a grand-slam home run in the seventh inning, and the Dodgers won 6–2.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/kyle-tucker-hits-grand-slam-dodgers-nearing-postseason-berth-clinch?game_pk=823817|title=Bit of trust, adjustments fuel Tucker's grand show as postseason nears for LA
|work=mlb.com|first=Perla|last=Paredes|date=September 11, 2026|access-date=September 12, 2026}}</ref> The Marlins took the next game, 4–3, to end the Dodgers eight-game winning streak.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/dodgers/news/dodgers-bats-go-quiet-against-marlins|title=Dodgers must wait at least a day before punching postseason ticket
|work=mlb.com|first=Perla|last=Paredes|date=September 12, 2026|access-date=September 13, 2026}}</ref> The series wrapped up with a 6–4 Marlins win. [[Josue De Paula]] hit a three-run home run in the fourth inning for his first major league hit as the Dodgers took a lead. However, the Marlins scored four runs in the ninth on a two-run double by [[Javier Sanoja]] followed by a home run by [[Agustín Ramírez (baseball)|Agustín Ramírez]] for the walk-off win.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/josue-de-paula-crushes-three-run-homer-for-first-career-hit?game_pk=823815|title=De Paula scorches no-doubter for 1st career hit before Marlins walk it off
|work=mlb.com|first=Perla|last=Paredes|date=September 13, 2026|access-date=September 14, 2026}}</ref> On September 14, the Dodgers began a series at [[Great American Ball Park]] against the Reds. Skubal struck out nine in seven shutout innings. The Dodgers won 4–1 and clinched their 14th straight postseason appearance, tying the [[Atlanta Braves]] all-time record ([[1991 Atlanta Braves season|1991]]-[[2005 Atlanta Braves season|2005]]).<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/dodgers-beat-reds-clinch-2026-playoff-berth?game_pk=824465|title=14 straight! Dodgers tie MLB record for most consecutive playoff clinches
|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=September 14, 2026|access-date=September 14, 2026}}</ref> The next day, Yamamoto allowed only one hit in seven scoreless innings while striking out seven. They won 4–0.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/yoshinobu-yamamoto-dominates-as-dodgers-shut-out-reds?game_pk=824466|title='Vintage Yama' locks in after shaky 1st as Dodgers shut out Reds
|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=September 15, 2026|access-date=September 16, 2026}}</ref> On September 16, Snell left the game after the first because of a groin injury and the Reds scored six runs off the Dodgers bullpen to beat them 6–2.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/dodgers/news/blake-snell-exits-start-against-reds-after-one-inning|title='Glad it's not worse': Snell's early exit (groin) complicates Dodgers' plans
|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=September 16, 2026|access-date=September 16, 2026}}</ref> The next day, Tucker had three hits, including a home run, and drove in three runs as the Dodgers won 8–2 to clinch the NL Western Division title, their fifth straight and 13th in 14 years.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/dodgers/news/dodgers-clinch-2026-nl-west-title|title=Tucker's near-cycle clinches LA's 5th straight NL West title, 13th in 14 years
|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=September 17, 2026|access-date=September 17, 2026}}</ref>

The Dodgers returned home on September 18 to play the [[San Francisco Giants]]. [[Tyler Glasnow]] struck out 10 batters in six innings, Mookie Betts had three hits, including two home runs, and the Dodgers won the opener 8–2.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/dodgers-hit-five-home-runs-to-beat-giants?game_pk=823898|title=Dodgers celebrate 20th anniversary of '4+1' game with 4-HR inning in rout
|work=mlb.com|first=Sarah|last=Wexler|date=September 18, 2026|access-date=September 19, 2026}}</ref> Betts had four more hits in the next game and [[Will Smith (catcher)|Will Smith]] homered in a 10–4 win.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/mookie-betts-has-4-hits-as-dodgers-beat-giants?game_pk=823899|title=Mookie's sizzling September continues with 4-hit game
|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=September 19, 2026|access-date=September 20, 2026}}</ref> The Dodgers took the next game, 4–1, as Sheehan struck out nine in {{fraction|5|1|3}} innings and Edman hit a home run.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/emmet-sheehan-strikes-out-9-giants-in-scoreless-long-relief?game_pk=823896|title=Sheehan posts impressive audition for postseason 'pen spot as LA sweeps SF
|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=September 20, 2026|access-date=September 20, 2026}}</ref> The [[San Diego Padres]] came to town for the final home stand of the regular season. The Dodgers scored four runs in the second and went on to win the opener, 7–0.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/roki-sasaki-activated-off-injured-list-to-join-dodgers-bullpen?game_pk=823897|title=Sasaki settles back into bullpen role with postseason approaching
|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=September 22, 2026|access-date=September 23, 2026}}</ref> A two-run home run by [[Manny Machado]] in the fourth inning gave the Padres a lead they never gave up, as they won the second game of the series, 5–1.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/manny-machado-homers-in-padres-win-over-dodgers?game_pk=823894|title=Machado's HR sets tone as Padres move one step closer to postseason berth
|work=mlb.com|first=AJ|last=Cassavell|date=September 23, 2026|access-date=September 24, 2026}}</ref> Glasnow struck out 11 in {{fraction|6|1|3}} innings in the final home game of the regular season, but the Padres scored three runs  in the seventh inning to win the game 4–2.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/padres-clinch-2026-postseason-berth?game_pk=823895|title=Padres clinch 3rd straight postseason berth as they seek 1st World Series title
|work=mlb.com|first=AJ|last=Cassavell|date=September 24, 2026|access-date=September 25, 2026}}</ref>

The Dodgers traveled to [[Oracle Park]] to wrap up the regular season with a three-game series against the Giants beginning on September 25. Skubal struck out 10 while only allowing three hits in seven scoreless innings while solo homers by Teoscar Hernández and [[Andy Pages]] gave the Dodgers their 2–0 win.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/tarik-skubal-throws-seven-scoreless-as-dodgers-shut-out-giants?game_pk=823167|title=Skubal cruises before Sasaki, Scott prep for postseason roles
|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=September 25, 2026|access-date=September 26, 2026}}</ref> The next day, a ninth inning [[Enrique Hernández (baseball)|Kiké Hernández]] home run gave the Dodgers a 4–3 win.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/blake-snell-solid-in-final-regular-season-tuneup-following-groin-injury?game_pk=823165|title=Snell shows he's ready to reprise role in LA's postseason rotation behemoth
|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=September 26, 2026|access-date=September 27, 2026}}</ref> The Dodgers won the regular season finale, 5–1, in 10 innings as Pages hit a three-run home run. It was the Dodgers 100th win of the season, the sixth time they had reached that mark under manager [[Dave Roberts (baseball manager)|Dave Roberts]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mlb.com/news/shohei-ohtani-hopes-to-shake-off-rust-heading-into-2026-postseason?game_pk=823164|title=Dodgers end season with 100th win, questions about Ohtani
|work=mlb.com|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=September 27, 2026|access-date=September 27, 2026}}</ref>

