===Division Series===
{{Main|2026 National League Division Series}}
The Dodgers hosted the [[2026 Atlanta Braves season|Atlanta Braves]] in the Division Series, starting on October 3 at [[Dodger Stadium]]. In the opening game, [[Tarik Skubal]] allowed only one run (a solo home run by [[Sean Murphy (baseball)|Sean Murphy]] on two hits in six innings while striking out seven. The Dodgers got two-run home runs from [[Teoscar Hernández]] and [[Kyle Tucker]] and a solo home run by [[Max Muncy]] and won the game 5–3.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/tarik-skubal-strikes-out-7-in-dodgers-nlds-game-1-start-vs-braves?game_pk=849828|title=Pumping 100 mph at 100° F, Skubal shows why he was hot Deadline commodity|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=October 3, 2026|access-date=October 4, 2026|work=mlb.com}}</ref> In the second game, [[Blake Snell]] was wild from the start, walking five and allowing only three his in {{fraction|3|1|3}} innings but only allowing one run. The Braves scored again on a triple by [[Michael Harris II]] in the fifth inning followed by a wild-pitch by reliever [[Kris Bubic]]. Harris drove in another run on a double in the seventh. [[Freddie Freeman]] and Muncy hit solo home runs but the Dodgers lost 3–2 to even the series.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/braves-win-nlds-game-2-2026?game_pk=849823|title=Braves follow manager's pitch-perfect plan to even series with Dodgers|first=Mark|last=Bowman|date=October 4, 2026|access-date=October 5, 2026|work=mlb.com}}</ref>

The series moved to [[Truist Park]] for games 3 and 4. The third game was a pitching matchup between [[Yoshinobu Yamamoto]] and [[Chris Sale]]. Yamamoto allowed only one run on four hits while striking out 10 batters in seven innings while Sale struck out seven in five innings but allowed seven hits. The Dodgers scored three runs in the fourth inning on a two-run [[Enrique Hernández (baseball)|Kiké Hernández]] home run and a solo home run by Freeman. They held on for the 3–1 victory to take the lead in the series.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/yoshinobu-yamamoto-10-strikeouts-nlds-game-3-2026?game_pk=849819|title=Yamamoto (10 K's) dazzles, putting Dodgers 1 win from return to NLCS|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=October 6, 2026|access-date=October 6, 2026|work=mlb.com}}</ref> In Game 4, [[Tyler Glasnow]] allowed only one run on one hit while striking out five in {{fraction|4|2|3}} innings. Both teams got first inning runs and then the Dodgers got two in the seventh when [[Andy Pages]] drove them in with a bases loaded hit. Muncy added another home run and the Dodgers finished off the series with a 4–1 win.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/dodgers-win-nlds-2026?game_pk=849822|title=Dodgers return to NLCS as Pages delivers clutch October moment|first=Sonja|last=Chen|date=October 7, 2026|access-date=October 7, 2026|work=mlb.com}}</ref>

===Championship Series===
{{Main|2026 National League Championship Series}}

The Dodgers played in their third straight NLCS and second straight against the [[2026 Milwaukee Brewers season|Milwaukee Brewers]]. The series began at [[American Family Field]] with a pitching matchup of [[Tarik Skubal]] vs [[Jacob Misiorowski]]. [[Andy Pages]] drove in a run in the second to give the Dodgers an early lead but [[Brice Turang]] hit a home run in the fourth to tie the game. [[Luis Lara]] drove in the go-ahead run the next inning to give the Brewers a 2–1 lead that held up the rest of the game.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/brewers-win-nlcs-game-1-2026?game_pk=849809|title=Brewers take Game 1 in NLCS rematch against defending champs|first=AJ|last=Cassavell|date=October 11, 2026|access-date=October 12, 2026|work=mlb.com}}</ref> [[Yoshinobu Yamamoto]] started the second game for the Dodgers, but he turned in his shortest outing in his post-season history, allowing three runs on four hits and two walks while only striking out one batter in {{fraction|2|2|3}} innings. [[William Contreras]] had three hits, including a home run, and drove in three runs as the Brewers won the game 7–4.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/dodgers/news/yoshinobu-yamamoto-exits-nlcs-game-2-vs-brewers-in-3rd-inning|title=Yamamoto's 12-pitch walk is turning point in his shortest postseason start|first=Christina|last=De Nicola|date=October 12, 2026|access-date=October 13, 2026|work=mlb.com}}</ref>

The series moved to [[Dodger Stadium]] for Game 3. [[Blake Snell]] started for the Dodgers, he cruised through the first five innings with nine strikeouts but allowed back to back home runs in the sixith inning to [[Jake Bauers]] and [[Garrett Mitchell (baseball)|Garrett Mitchell]] to give the Brewers a lead they never relinquished as they went on to win 6–2.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/brewers-win-nlcs-game-3-2026?game_pk=849811|title=Brewers keep October magic rolling, on cusp of 1st World Series in 44 years|first=AJ|last=Cassavell|date=October 14, 2026|access-date=October 15, 2026|work=mlb.com}}</ref> In Game 4, [[Shohei Ohtani]] hit a three-run home run in the second inning to give the Dodgers an early 4–0 lead but it didn't last. The Brewers got a two-run double by Bauers in the fourth inning off[[Tyler Glasnow]] and tied the game the following inning on a RBI single by [[David Hamilton (baseball)|David Hamilton]] and a bases load walk issued by [[Alex Vesia]]. A solo homer by Mitchell off [[Tanner Scott]] the next inning gave the Brewers the lead they would never surrender in a 6–4 win to finish off the sweep and end the Dodgers' quest for a three-peat.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mlb.com/news/brewers-win-nlcs-2026?game_pk=849810|title=Brewers sweep champs, reach World Series for first time in 44 years|first=AJ|last=Cassavell|date=October 15, 2026|access-date=October 15, 2026|work=mlb.com}}</ref>

